---
// QuiverAI's story, from Nicklas Scharpff's answers (2026-10-08); the page
// counts are from docs.quiver.ai's sitemap.
import { NEW_TAB_ATTRS } from "blume/core/new-tab.ts";
import Quote from "../_customers/Quote.astro";
import StoryPage from "../_customers/StoryPage.astro";
import { storyById } from "../_customers/customers.ts";
---

<StoryPage story={storyById("quiverai")}>
  <p>
    <a href="https://quiver.ai" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>QuiverAI</a> is a frontier
    AI lab building models for graphic design. Its flagship, Arrow, is a model
    for SVG generation: it turns a prompt into vector artwork, vectorizes
    images, and edits and animates existing SVGs. QuiverAI serves Arrow in the
    QuiverAI App and over the QuiverAI API, the main gateway for its business
    customers, used by companies like Paper, Flora, Vercel, and ComfyUI.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its docs cover all of it: the App, the API platform, the API reference,
    and the MCP server and plugins that bring Arrow to Codex and Cursor.
  </p>

  <h2>How the docs started</h2>
  <p>
    QuiverAI's docs started on Mintlify, and Mintlify worked very well for the
    team. But as QuiverAI wanted more control over how its docs looked, it
    needed more room than Mintlify gave it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The choice seemed to be Mintlify or a custom site. QuiverAI wanted both: the
    freedom of building its own, with the professional feel and the support
    for integrations that Mintlify had.
  </p>

  <h2>Building Blume together</h2>
  <p>
    Earlier in 2026, Nicklas Scharpff shared those concerns with Hayden
    Bleasel, just as Hayden was starting on Blume. Hayden brought QuiverAI into
    many of Blume's technical decisions, both to make sure Blume fit what
    QuiverAI needed and as a stand-in for other companies at a similar scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    That meant QuiverAI's workflows and existing docs worked from day one. The
    migration was barely more than one CLI command,{" "}
    <code>blume migrate mintlify</code>, plus a prompt or two to refine the
    result, and the docs were in production in less than a day.
  </p>

  <h2>Docs that look like QuiverAI</h2>
  <p>
    What changed most was control over the design. Blume's{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/theming#design-tokens">design tokens</a>{" "}
    carry QuiverAI's visual identity into every page, and{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/customization#component-overrides"
      >component overrides</a
    > let the team replace any built-in component with its own.
  </p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/nicklas-scharpff.webp"
    name="Nicklas Scharpff"
    role="Co-founder, QuiverAI"
  >
    The freedom to override any UI component, and how flexibly Blume adapts
    design tokens, made adapting our vision for the docs very straightforward.
  </Quote>

  <p>
    The same flexibility took Blume past the public docs. It's now also the
    backbone of QuiverAI's internal design system documentation.
  </p>

  <h2>Ready for agents</h2>
  <p>
    QuiverAI's product reaches agents through its MCP server and plugins, and
    its docs reach them too, with no extra work. Every page has a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/markdown">Markdown version</a>, the site
    publishes <a href="/docs/discoverability/llms-txt">llms.txt</a> and
    llms-full.txt, and an AI catalog lists its agent-facing resources.
  </p>
  <p>
    The API reference builds from QuiverAI's OpenAPI spec with{" "}
    <a href="/docs/references/openapi"><code>openapi()</code></a>: every
    endpoint, from text to SVG to SVG animation, gets a page with its
    schemas, code samples, and a Try it panel.
  </p>

  <h2>The results</h2>
  <p>
    QuiverAI went from Mintlify to production in less than a day, and Blume now
    runs two of its docs sites: the public docs and the internal design
    system.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">What</th>
        <th scope="col">Number</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Public docs pages</th><td>35</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">API endpoint pages</th><td>8, from an OpenAPI spec</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Time to production</th><td>Less than a day</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Docs sites on Blume</th><td>2, public and internal</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For teams weighing the same move, Nicklas keeps it short.</p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/nicklas-scharpff.webp"
    name="Nicklas Scharpff"
    role="Co-founder, QuiverAI"
  >
    Just try it out. It's so simple, and you get to an MVP super fast.
  </Quote>

  <p>
    The new docs are live at{" "}
    <a href="https://docs.quiver.ai" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>docs.quiver.ai</a>. To
    make the same move, follow the{" "}
    <a href="/guides/migrate-from-mintlify">Mintlify migration guide</a>.
  </p>
</StoryPage>
